Bash is a commnad language interpreter that provides an environemnt in which users can execute commands and run applications. 


Enviornmental Setup

Accessing the BASH shell: 

  Kali & MacOS use Z Shell by default, so when you open a new terminal window, you'll have to enter exec bash to switch to a bash shell before you run commands. 

  If you want to change your default shellto bash so you don't have to manually switch shells, you can use the chsh -s /bin/bash command. 

  If you're running Windows, you can use the Windows subsystem for Linux(WSL), which lets you run Linux distributions and access a bash environment. 

  An alternative to WSL is Cygwin, which emulates a Linux environmentby providing a colleciton of Linux utilites and system-call functionalities. 

Installing a Text Editor 

  To start writing scripts, you'll need a text editor, preferably one with handy features such as syntax highlighting built in. 

  You can choose between terminal-based editors or graphical user interface-based text editors. 

    Terminal-based text editors: vi or GNU nano

    Graphical text editor: Sublime text. 

Exploring the Shell

  Enter the following command to verify that bash is available on your system: 

    $ bash --version

  Checking Environment Variables

    When running in terminal, bash loads a set of environment variables with every new session that gets invoked. 

    To see the list of environment variables set by bash, run the env command directly from the shell

    $ env

